== Examples: orthonormal series, the torus and the sphere <sec:mercer-examples>

In all examples of this section the kernel is _constructed_ from a prescribed orthonormal system, so the
eigensystem of $T_K$ is known in advance. This is the typical way of designing kernels with prescribed
smoothness.

#example(title: [Orthonormal-series kernels])[
  Let $(X, d)$ be a compact metric space and $mu$ a finite Borel measure with $supp mu = X$. Let
  $(phi_k)_(k in I)$, $I$ countable, be continuous functions that are orthonormal in $L^2(mu)$, and let
  $w_k >= 0$ be weights such that $sum_(k in I) w_k abs(phi_k (x))^2$ converges _uniformly_ on $X$ (that is,
  for every $epsilon > 0$ there is a finite $F subset I$ with $sum_(k in.not F) w_k abs(phi_k (x))^2 < epsilon$ for
  all $x$). A simple sufficient condition is $sum_k w_k norm(phi_k)_oo^2 < oo$. Define
  $ K(x, y) := sum_(k in I) w_k phi_k (x) overline(phi_k (y)), quad x, y in X . $
  + The series converges absolutely and uniformly on $X times X$, and $K$ is a continuous positive definite
    kernel.
  + $T_K g = sum_(k in I) w_k ip(g, phi_k)_(L^2(mu)) phi_k$. The nonzero eigenvalues of $T_K$ are the positive
    $w_k$; an eigensystem is given by $(w_k, phi_k)_(k in I_+)$, $I_+ := {k : w_k > 0}$, enumerated so that the
    weights are nonincreasing; and $Ker T_K = {phi_k : k in I_+}^perp$.
  + $H_K = {sum_(k in I_+) c_k phi_k : sum_(k in I_+) abs(c_k)^2 \/ w_k < oo}$ with
    $norm(f)_(H_K)^2 = sum_(k in I_+) abs(c_k)^2 \/ w_k$, where $c_k = ip(f, phi_k)_(L^2(mu))$.

  In particular, if $(phi_k)$ is an orthonormal _basis_ of $L^2(mu)$ and all $w_k > 0$, then $Ker T_K = {0}$ and
  $H_K$ consists of the continuous representatives of the classes $f in L^2(mu)$ with
  $sum_k abs(ip(f, phi_k))^2 \/ w_k < oo$.
] <ex:mercer-series>

#proof[
  (i) For a finite set $F subset I$ with $sum_(k in.not F) w_k abs(phi_k (z))^2 < epsilon$ for all $z$, the
  Cauchy–Schwarz inequality gives
  $sum_(k in.not F) w_k abs(phi_k (x)) abs(phi_k (y)) <= epsilon$ for all $x, y$. Hence the family is absolutely
  summable, uniformly on $X times X$, and $K$ is continuous as a uniform limit of continuous finite partial
  sums. For $x_1, ..., x_n in X$ and $c in KK^n$,
  $ sum_(i, l) overline(c_i) c_l K(x_i, x_l) = sum_(k in I) w_k (sum_i overline(c_i) phi_k (x_i)) (sum_l c_l overline(phi_k (x_l))) = sum_(k in I) w_k abs(sum_i overline(c_i) phi_k (x_i))^2 >= 0, $
  and $K$ is Hermitian, so $K$ is positive definite.

  (ii) For fixed $x$, the series defining $K(x, y)$ converges uniformly in $y$, and $g in L^2(mu) subset.eq L^1(mu)$
  because $mu$ is finite. We may therefore integrate term by term:
  $ integral_X K(x, y) g(y) dif mu(y) = sum_k w_k phi_k (x) integral_X overline(phi_k (y)) g(y) dif mu(y) = sum_k w_k ip(g, phi_k) phi_k (x) . $
  Hence $T_K phi_k = w_k phi_k$, and $T_K g = 0$ for $g perp {phi_k : k in I_+}$. If $T_K g = w g$ with $w > 0$,
  then $g = w^(-1) T_K g$ lies in $overline(spn){phi_k : k in I_+}$ and
  $w ip(g, phi_k) = w_k ip(g, phi_k)$ for all $k$, so $g in overline(spn){phi_k : w_k = w}$. Therefore the
  eigenspace of $w$ is $overline(spn){phi_k : w_k = w}$ (finite-dimensional since $T_K$ is compact), and
  $(phi_k)_(k in I_+)$ is an orthonormal basis of $(Ker T_K)^perp$ consisting of eigenvectors. Since only
  finitely many $w_k$ exceed any given $epsilon > 0$ (compactness), they can be enumerated in nonincreasing
  order.

  (iii) This is @prop:mercer-rkhs-spectral (i) with $a_k = c_k \/ sqrt(w_k)$, together with (ii) of that
  proposition.
]

For instance, the Legendre polynomials $P_k$ on $X = [-1, 1]$ with $mu = dif x \/ 2$ give the orthonormal
basis $phi_k = sqrt(2k + 1) P_k$ with $norm(phi_k)_oo = sqrt(2k + 1)$. Any weights with
$sum_k w_k (2k + 1) < oo$ produce a continuous positive definite kernel whose RKHS consists of the
functions with Legendre coefficients $c_k$ satisfying $sum_k abs(c_k)^2 \/ w_k < oo$. The polynomial weights
$w_k = (k + 1)^(-s)$, $s > 2$, give the Legendre example of @ex:rkhs-kernels (there with the Lebesgue
measure, which only rescales the $phi_k$ and the eigenvalues by a factor $2$): the larger $s$, the smoother
the functions. Geometric weights $w_k = rho^k$, $0 < rho < 1$, force geometric decay of the coefficients,
and one can show that such functions extend holomorphically to a neighbourhood of $[-1, 1]$ in $CC$.
In @sec:rkhs the RKHS of an orthonormal-series kernel was computed directly from the series; here we see in
addition that the $phi_k$ are exactly the eigenfunctions of $T_K$, so that such kernels are the
prototypes of Mercer's theorem.

=== The torus

#example(title: [Translation-invariant kernels on the torus])[
  Let $d in NN$ and $TT^d := RR^d \/ (2 pi ZZ)^d$. We identify functions on $TT^d$ with functions on $RR^d$
  that are $2 pi$-periodic in each variable. With the metric
  $d(x, y) := min_(m in ZZ^d) abs(x - y - 2 pi m)$, $TT^d$ is a compact metric space. Let $mu$ be the
  normalized Lebesgue measure, $dif mu = (2 pi)^(-d) dif x$ on $[0, 2 pi)^d$; it is translation invariant and has
  full support. The functions
  $ e_k (x) := e^(i k dot x), quad k in ZZ^d, $
  form an orthonormal basis of $L^2(TT^d, mu)$. Orthonormality is a direct computation. For completeness,
  the trigonometric polynomials form a subalgebra of $C(TT^d)$ that is closed under conjugation, contains
  the constants and separates the points of $TT^d$, so they are dense in $C(TT^d)$ by the Stone–Weierstrass
  theorem (@thm:app-stone-weierstrass); and $C(TT^d)$ is dense in $L^2(mu)$. For $f in L^2(mu)$ let
  $hat(f)(k) := ip(f, e_k)_(L^2(mu)) = integral f(x) e^(-i k dot x) dif mu(x)$ be its Fourier coefficients.

  Let $w = (w_k)_(k in ZZ^d)$ with $w_k >= 0$ and $sum_k w_k < oo$, and define
  $ K(x, y) := sum_(k in ZZ^d) w_k e^(i k dot (x - y)) = kappa(x - y), quad kappa(t) := sum_(k in ZZ^d) w_k e^(i k dot t) . $
  Since $abs(e_k (x))^2 = 1$, this is an orthonormal-series kernel as in @ex:mercer-series (the series
  $sum_k w_k abs(e_k (x))^2 = sum_k w_k$ converges uniformly). Hence:
  + $K$ is a continuous, positive definite, translation-invariant kernel. It is real-valued if and only if
    $w_k = w_(-k)$ for all $k$, and then $K(x, y) = w_0 + 2 sum_(k in Z_+) w_k cos(k dot (x - y))$, where
    $Z_+$ contains exactly one element of each pair ${k, -k}$, $k != 0$.
  + The eigensystem of $T_K$ is $(w_k, e_k)_(w_k > 0)$: $T_K$ multiplies Fourier coefficients by $w_k$,
    $(T_K g)^and (k) = w_k hat(g)(k)$. Mercer's expansion is the Fourier series of $kappa$.
  + $H_K = {f in C(TT^d) : hat(f)(k) = 0 "whenever" w_k = 0, space sum_(w_k > 0) abs(hat(f)(k))^2 \/ w_k < oo}$
    with $norm(f)_(H_K)^2 = sum_(w_k > 0) abs(hat(f)(k))^2 \/ w_k$.
  + *(Trigonometric polynomials.)* If $F := {k : w_k > 0}$ is finite, e.g. $F = {k : abs(k)_oo <= n}$, then
    $H_K = spn{e_k : k in F}$ is a space of trigonometric polynomials: a band-limited RKHS.
  + *(Sobolev spaces.)* For $w_k = (1 + abs(k)^2)^(-s)$ we have $sum_k w_k < oo$ if and only if $s > d\/2$
    (@ex:mercer-exercise-sobolev), and then
    $ H_K = H^s (TT^d) := {f in L^2(mu) : sum_(k in ZZ^d) (1 + abs(k)^2)^s abs(hat(f)(k))^2 < oo}, quad
      norm(f)_(H_K)^2 = sum_(k in ZZ^d) (1 + abs(k)^2)^s abs(hat(f)(k))^2, $
    where each class in $H^s (TT^d)$ is identified with its (unique) continuous representative. For weights
    with $c_1 (1 + abs(k)^2)^(-s) <= w_k <= c_2 (1 + abs(k)^2)^(-s)$, $H_K = H^s (TT^d)$ with an equivalent norm.
] <ex:mercer-torus>

The Sobolev case deserves a closer look. That every element of $H^s (TT^d)$ has a continuous
representative when $s > d\/2$ is the Sobolev embedding theorem on the torus; here it comes for free, since
$H_K subset.eq C(TT^d)$. We even get the explicit bound $norm(f)_oo <= sqrt(kappa(0)) norm(f)_(H^s)$ from
$abs(f(x)) <= sqrt(K(x, x)) norm(f)_(H_K)$. For $d = 1$ and $s = 1$, the classical formula
$sum_(k in ZZ) e^(i k t) \/ (1 + k^2) = pi cosh(pi - t) \/ sinh(pi)$ for $t in [0, 2 pi]$ gives the explicit
kernel
$ K(x, y) = pi / sinh(pi) cosh(pi - abs(x - y)), quad abs(x - y) <= pi, $
extended periodically, and for continuously differentiable (or, more generally, absolutely continuous with
square-integrable derivative) periodic $f$, integration by parts gives $(f')^and (k) = i k hat(f)(k)$, so
$ norm(f)_(H_K)^2 = sum_k (1 + k^2) abs(hat(f)(k))^2 = norm(f)_(L^2(mu))^2 + norm(f')_(L^2(mu))^2 . $
Here the regularizer $lambda norm(f)_(H_K)^2$ is literally a penalty on the derivative. Regularized least
squares with such kernels leads to periodic smoothing splines @wahba1990. @fig:mercer-torus-kernels
compares this kernel with a band-limited one.

