Mercer's theorem also shows that _every_ continuous positive definite translation-invariant kernel on the
torus is of the form of @ex:mercer-torus. This is the torus version of Bochner's theorem on positive
definite functions.

#proposition[
  Let $kappa: TT^d -> CC$ be continuous such that $K(x, y) := kappa(x - y)$ is a positive definite kernel on
  $TT^d$. Then $hat(kappa)(k) >= 0$ for all $k in ZZ^d$, $sum_k hat(kappa)(k) = kappa(0) < oo$, and
  $kappa(t) = sum_k hat(kappa)(k) e^(i k dot t)$ with absolute and uniform convergence.
] <prop:mercer-torus-bochner>

#proof[
  By translation invariance of $mu$ (substitute $t = x - y$),
  $ (T_K e_k)(x) = integral kappa(x - y) e^(i k dot y) dif mu(y) = e^(i k dot x) integral kappa(t) e^(-i k dot t) dif mu(t) = hat(kappa)(k) e_k (x) . $
  So the bounded operator $T_K$ acts diagonally on the orthonormal basis $(e_k)$, i.e.
  $T_K g = sum_k hat(kappa)(k) hat(g)(k) e_k$ by continuity. Positivity of $T_K$
  (@thm:mercer-integral-operator (iii)) gives $hat(kappa)(k) = ip(T_K e_k, e_k) >= 0$. As in the proof of
  @ex:mercer-series (ii), the pairs $(hat(kappa)(k), e_k)$ with $hat(kappa)(k) > 0$, enumerated in
  nonincreasing order, form an eigensystem of $T_K$. Mercer's theorem (@thm:mercer) yields
  $ kappa(x - y) = sum_(k in ZZ^d) hat(kappa)(k) e^(i k dot x) e^(-i k dot y) $
  absolutely and uniformly, and $sum_k hat(kappa)(k) = integral K(x, x) dif mu(x) = kappa(0)$. Put $y = 0$.
]

=== The sphere

The sphere is the prototype of a curved compact space, and here the role of the Fourier basis is played
by the spherical harmonics, the eigenfunctions of the Laplace–Beltrami operator. We recall the facts we
need; proofs can be found in @atkinson2012[Ch. 2–3].

Let $d >= 2$ and $SS^d := {x in RR^(d+1) : abs(x) = 1}$ with the geodesic distance
$rho(x, y) = arccos(x dot y)$ and the surface measure $sigma$, whose total mass is
$abs(SS^d) = 2 pi^((d+1)\/2) \/ Gamma((d+1)\/2)$ (e.g. $abs(SS^2) = 4 pi$). The case $d = 1$ is the circle
$TT$ treated above.

*The Laplace–Beltrami operator.* On a compact Riemannian manifold $(M, g)$ it is given in local
coordinates by
$ Delta_M f = 1/sqrt(abs(g)) sum_(i, l = 1)^(dim M) partial_i (sqrt(abs(g)) g^(i l) partial_l f), $
where $abs(g) = det(g_(i l))$ and $(g^(i l)) = (g_(i l))^(-1)$. For $M = SS^d$ it can be read off from the
Laplacian of $RR^(d+1)$ in polar coordinates $x = r xi$, $r > 0$, $xi in SS^d$:
$ Delta_(RR^(d+1)) = partial_r^2 + d/r partial_r + 1/r^2 Delta_(SS^d) . $
The operator $-Delta_M$ is unbounded, symmetric and nonnegative on $C^oo (M) subset L^2(M)$, and it is
essentially self-adjoint there. It is _not_ compact (it is not even bounded), but its resolvent
$(I - Delta_M)^(-1)$ is a compact, positive, self-adjoint operator on $L^2(M)$. The spectral theorem
(@thm:app-spectral), applied to the resolvent, therefore yields an orthonormal basis of $L^2(M)$ of
(smooth) eigenfunctions of $-Delta_M$, with eigenvalues $0 = nu_0 <= nu_1 <= ... -> oo$.

*Spherical harmonics.* A spherical harmonic of degree $k in NN_0$ is the restriction to $SS^d$ of a
polynomial $p$ on $RR^(d+1)$ that is homogeneous of degree $k$ and harmonic, $Delta p = 0$. Let
$cal(H)_k$ denote the space of these functions (not to be confused with an RKHS $H_K$). Writing
$p(r xi) = r^k Y(xi)$ and applying the polar formula,
$ 0 = Delta p = (k(k - 1) + d k) r^(k-2) Y + r^(k-2) Delta_(SS^d) Y, quad "so" quad -Delta_(SS^d) Y = k(k + d - 1) Y . $
The following facts are standard @atkinson2012:
+ $dim cal(H)_k = h_d (k) := (2k + d - 1) ((k + d - 2)!) / (k! (d-1)!)$; e.g. $h_d (0) = 1$,
  $h_d (1) = d + 1$, and $h_2 (k) = 2k + 1$.
+ The spaces $cal(H)_k$ are mutually orthogonal in $L^2(SS^d, sigma)$, and $L^2(SS^d, sigma)$ is the
  closure of $plus.o.big_(k >= 0) cal(H)_k$. They are exactly the eigenspaces of $-Delta_(SS^d)$.
+ *(Addition theorem.)* For every orthonormal basis $(Y_(k, l))_(l = 1)^(h_d (k))$ of $cal(H)_k$,
  $ sum_(l = 1)^(h_d (k)) Y_(k, l)(x) overline(Y_(k, l)(y)) = (h_d (k)) / abs(SS^d) P_k^((d)) (x dot y), quad x, y in SS^d, $ <eq:mercer-addition>
  where $P_k^((d))$ is the Legendre polynomial of degree $k$ in dimension $d + 1$ (denoted $P_(k, d+1)$ in
  @atkinson2012), normalized by $P_k^((d))(1) = 1$ and satisfying $abs(P_k^((d))(t)) <= 1$ on $[-1, 1]$. It
  is proportional to the Gegenbauer polynomial $C_k^(((d-1)\/2))$ and to the Jacobi polynomial
  $P_k^((d\/2 - 1, space d\/2 - 1))$. For $d = 2$ it is the classical Legendre polynomial $P_k$.

#align(center, block(breakable: false, table(
  columns: 4,
  align: center + horizon,
  stroke: (x, y) => (top: if y == 1 { 0.6pt } else { 0pt }),
  table.header([degree $k$], [eigenvalue of $-Delta_(SS^d)$], [$dim cal(H)_k$ on $SS^d$], [$dim cal(H)_k$ on $SS^2$]),
  [$0$], [$0$], [$1$], [$1$],
  [$1$], [$d$], [$d + 1$], [$3$],
  [$2$], [$2(d + 1)$], [$d(d + 3)\/2$], [$5$],
  [$k$], [$k(k + d - 1)$], [$h_d (k)$], [$2k + 1$],
)))

#example(title: [Zonal kernels on the sphere])[
  Let $d >= 2$, $mu = sigma$ on $SS^d$, and for each $k$ let $(Y_(k, l))_(l=1)^(h_d (k))$ be an orthonormal
  basis of $cal(H)_k$. By (ii), the whole family $(Y_(k, l))_(k, l)$ is an orthonormal basis of $L^2(SS^d)$
  consisting of continuous functions. Choose weights that depend only on the degree, $w_(k, l) = w_k >= 0$,
  with
  $ sum_(k >= 0) w_k h_d (k) < oo, $
  and define the orthonormal-series kernel
  $ K(x, y) := sum_(k >= 0) w_k sum_(l = 1)^(h_d (k)) Y_(k, l)(x) overline(Y_(k, l)(y)) = sum_(k >= 0) w_k (h_d (k)) / abs(SS^d) P_k^((d)) (x dot y) . $
  By the addition theorem, $sum_l abs(Y_(k, l)(x))^2 = h_d (k) \/ abs(SS^d)$ for every $x$, so the tails of
  $sum_(k, l) w_k abs(Y_(k, l)(x))^2$ do not depend on $x$, and the uniform-convergence hypothesis of
  @ex:mercer-series holds. Therefore:
  + $K$ is a continuous positive definite kernel that depends only on $x dot y = cos rho(x, y)$. Such
    kernels are called *zonal*; they are invariant under rotations, $K(Q x, Q y) = K(x, y)$ for orthogonal
    $Q$. The kernel does not depend on the choice of the bases $(Y_(k, l))_l$.
  + $T_K Y = w_k Y$ for every $Y in cal(H)_k$ (a form of the Funk–Hecke formula below). The nonzero
    eigenvalues are the positive $w_k$; if the positive $w_k$ are pairwise distinct, $w_k$ has
    multiplicity exactly $h_d (k)$.
  + Writing $f = sum_k f_k$ with $f_k in cal(H)_k$ the orthogonal projections in $L^2(sigma)$, the space
    $H_K$ consists of the continuous representatives of the classes
    $ {f in L^2(sigma) : f_k = 0 "whenever" w_k = 0, space sum_(w_k > 0) norm(f_k)_(L^2(sigma))^2 / w_k < oo}, quad "and" quad
      norm(f)_(H_K)^2 = sum_(w_k > 0) norm(f_k)_(L^2(sigma))^2 / w_k . $
  + *(Sobolev spaces.)* Since $c_d k^(d-1) <= h_d (k) <= C_d k^(d-1)$ for $k >= 1$, the weights
    $w_k = (1 + k(k + d - 1))^(-s)$ satisfy the summability condition if and only if $s > d\/2$. The
    resulting RKHS is the Sobolev space $H^s (SS^d)$, defined through the eigen-expansion of
    $-Delta_(SS^d)$, with norm $norm(f)^2 = sum_k (1 + k(k + d - 1))^s norm(f_k)^2$. The choice
    $w_k = e^(-t k(k + d - 1))$, $t > 0$, gives the *heat kernel* of the sphere.
] <ex:mercer-sphere>

The same construction works on any compact Riemannian manifold $M$: with an orthonormal eigenbasis
$(phi_j)$ of $-Delta_M$ and eigenvalues $nu_j$, one considers $K(x, y) = sum_j w(nu_j) phi_j (x) overline(phi_j (y))$
for a decreasing function $w$, such as $w(nu) = (1 + nu)^(-s)$ (Sobolev kernels, $s > dim M \/ 2$) or
$w(nu) = e^(-t nu)$ (heat kernels). Verifying the uniform-convergence hypothesis then requires bounds on
the eigenfunctions, which are available but beyond our scope. The sphere is special because the addition
theorem makes these bounds trivial.

#context-note[
  Conversely, a classical theorem of Schoenberg @schoenberg1942 states that a continuous function
  $kappa: [-1, 1] -> RR$ gives a positive definite kernel $kappa(x dot y)$ on $SS^d$ for a fixed $d$ if and
  only if $kappa = sum_k a_k P_k^((d))$ with $a_k >= 0$ and $sum_k a_k < oo$. This is the spherical analogue
  of @prop:mercer-torus-bochner, and it can be proved in the same way, using the *Funk–Hecke formula*
  @atkinson2012: for $Y in cal(H)_k$,
  $ integral_(SS^d) kappa(x dot y) Y(y) dif sigma(y) = abs(SS^(d-1)) (integral_(-1)^1 kappa(t) P_k^((d))(t) (1 - t^2)^((d-2)\/2) dif t) Y(x) . $
  Dot-product kernels such as $e^(x dot y)$ or $(1 + x dot y)^m$, restricted to the sphere, are zonal, and
  their eigenvalues can be computed with this formula. Spline smoothing on the sphere is treated in
  @wahba1990.
]

== Summary

- For a measurable kernel with separable RKHS and $abs(K)_p < oo$, the RKHS embeds continuously into
  $L^p (mu)$ with norm at most $abs(K)_p$. The (suitably identified) adjoint of the embedding is the
  integral operator $g |-> integral g(y) K_y dif mu(y)$, and density of $H_K$ in $L^p$ is equivalent to
  injectivity of this operator (@thm:mercer-lp-embedding).
- For $p = 2$: $S_K$ is Hilbert–Schmidt with $norm(S_K)_("HS") = abs(K)_2$, and $T_K = S_K^* S_K$ is the
  integral operator with kernel $K$ on $L^2(mu)$. It is compact, positive, self-adjoint and trace class, with
  $tr T_K = integral K(x, x) dif mu(x)$ and $norm(T_K)_("HS") = norm(K)_(L^2(mu times.o mu))$
  (@thm:mercer-integral-operator, @prop:mercer-ae-expansion).
- The eigenfunctions $e_j$ of $T_K$ have canonical representatives $lambda_j^(-1) S_K e_j in H_K$, and
  $(sqrt(lambda_j) e_j)$ is an orthonormal basis of $(Ker iota)^perp subset.eq H_K$
  (@prop:mercer-eigenfunctions). In general the kernel expansion in these functions holds only almost
  everywhere.
- *Mercer's theorem* (@thm:mercer): for continuous positive definite $K$ on a compact metric space and a
  finite measure of full support, $K(x, y) = sum_j lambda_j e_j (x) overline(e_j (y))$ absolutely and uniformly.
  Full support and positivity are both essential. Positivity of $T_K$ is equivalent to positive
  definiteness of $K$ (@lem:mercer-positivity).
- *Spectral picture* (@prop:mercer-rkhs-spectral): $H_K = {sum_j a_j sqrt(lambda_j) e_j : a in ell^2}$ and
  $norm(f)_(H_K)^2 = sum_j abs(ip(f, e_j)_(L^2))^2 \/ lambda_j$. Moreover $iota(H_K) = Ran T_K^(1\/2)$, and
  $T_K^(1\/2)$ is an isometry from $(Ker T_K)^perp$ onto $H_K$. The RKHS is a smoothness space governed by the eigenvalue decay, and regularization acts
  as the spectral filter $lambda_j \/ (lambda_j + lambda)$.
- On the torus, orthonormal-series kernels are Fourier multipliers, and their RKHSs range from
  trigonometric polynomials to Sobolev spaces $H^s (TT^d)$, $s > d\/2$. On the sphere, zonal kernels play
  the same role with spherical harmonics.

Next, in @sec:univ, we ask the opposite question: rather than describing how _small_ $H_K$ is, we ask when
it is _large_, namely dense in $C(X)$. Fourier kernels with all $w_k > 0$ will be our first examples of such
universal kernels (@ex:univ-fourier).

== Notes and further reading

The theory of integral equations with symmetric kernels goes back to Hilbert and Schmidt in the first
decade of the twentieth century. Mercer @mercer1909 proved the theorem that bears his name for continuous
kernels of "positive type" on a compact interval with Lebesgue measure. A classical proof that does not
use RKHSs is outlined in @ex:mercer-exercise-classical. The systematic theory of reproducing
kernels, which gives the much shorter proof presented here, is due to Aronszajn @aronszajn1950.

Our treatment of the embedding of $H_K$ into $L^p (mu)$, the operators $S_K$ and $T_K$, and Mercer's theorem
follows @steinwart2008[Ch. 4], where measurability issues are also discussed in detail. Mercer's theorem
has been extended to non-compact spaces and more general measures; the almost-everywhere expansion of
@prop:mercer-ae-expansion is a first step in that direction, and @steinwart2012mercer give a systematic
account of the interplay between measures, kernels and RKHSs in this generality. The integral operator
$T_K$ (denoted $L_K$ there) plays a central role in the approximation-theoretic approach to learning
theory of @cucker2007, where the regularity of the target function is expressed through the ranges of
powers of $T_K$, in the spirit of @ex:mercer-filter. An operator-theoretic introduction to RKHSs,
including Mercer's theorem, is @paulsen2016.

For the connections with probability, including Gaussian processes and Karhunen–Loève expansions, see
@berlinet2004 and, from the machine-learning side, @rasmussen2006. Kernel PCA was introduced in
@schoelkopf1998 (see also @schoelkopf2002), and the Nyström extension of @ex:mercer-nystrom was popularized
as a tool for speeding up kernel machines by @williams2001. Historically, Mercer's theorem was the
justification of the kernel trick in the SVM literature @vapnik1998 @cortes1995.

The spectral theorem for compact self-adjoint operators and the theory of Hilbert–Schmidt and trace-class
operators are covered in @werner2018 and @brezis2011. The Fourier-analytic side of the torus examples,
including the existence of continuous functions with divergent Fourier series used in
@rem:mercer-indefinite, is in @rudin1987. Spherical harmonics, the addition theorem and the Funk–Hecke
formula are treated in detail in @atkinson2012. Smoothing splines on the circle and on the sphere, which
are regularized least squares estimators in the RKHSs of @ex:mercer-torus and @ex:mercer-sphere, are the
subject of @wahba1990.

== Exercises

#exercise(title: [Norm of the embedding])[
  In the setting of @thm:mercer-integral-operator, show that $norm(iota) = sqrt(lambda_1)$ if $T_K != 0$ (and
  $iota = 0$ otherwise), where $lambda_1 = norm(T_K)$ is the largest eigenvalue. Deduce that
  $norm(iota) <= abs(K)_2$, with equality if and only if $T_K$ has rank at most one.

  _Hint:_ $norm(iota)^2 = norm(S_K)^2 = norm(T_K)$ by @thm:mercer-integral-operator (iii), and compare
  $lambda_1$ with $sum_j lambda_j = abs(K)_2^2$.
] <ex:mercer-exercise-norm>

#exercise(title: [Size of eigenvalues and eigenfunctions])[
  (a) Let $lambda_1 >= lambda_2 >= ... >= 0$ with $sum_j lambda_j < oo$. Show that $j lambda_j -> 0$. _Hint:_
  $floor(j\/2) lambda_j <= sum_(j\/2 < i <= j) lambda_i$.
  (b) In the Mercer setting, show that $norm(e_j)_oo <= abs(K)_oo \/ sqrt(lambda_j)$, and that
  $sum_j lambda_j norm(e_j)_oo^2$ may be infinite even though $sum_j lambda_j abs(e_j (x))^2 = K(x, x)$ is
  bounded. _Hint for the second part:_ use @ex:mercer-series with $phi_k$ having disjoint supports.
] <ex:mercer-exercise-decay>

#exercise(title: [Brownian motion])[
  Let $X = [0, 1]$ with Lebesgue measure and $K(x, y) = min{x, y}$.
  (a) Show that $u = T_K g$ satisfies
  $u(x) = integral_0^x y g(y) dif y + x integral_x^1 g(y) dif y$, hence $u(0) = 0$, $u'(1) = 0$ and $u'' = -g$
  for continuous $g$.
  (b) Deduce that the eigenpairs are $lambda_j = ((j - 1\/2) pi)^(-2)$,
  $e_j (x) = sqrt(2) sin((j - 1\/2) pi x)$, $j in NN$.
  (c) Write down Mercer's expansion of $min{x, y}$ and verify the trace formula using
  $sum_(j >= 1) (2j - 1)^(-2) = pi^2 \/ 8$.
  (d) Show that $(sqrt(lambda_j) e_j)'$ is an orthonormal basis of $L^2[0, 1]$, and conclude with
  @prop:mercer-rkhs-spectral that $H_K$ is the space
  ${f : f(0) = 0, f "absolutely continuous", f' in L^2}$ with $norm(f)_(H_K) = norm(f')_(L^2)$ of
  @prop:rkhs-sobolev. (With independent standard normal $xi_j$, the series $sum_j xi_j sqrt(lambda_j) e_j (t)$ is the
  Karhunen–Loève expansion of Brownian motion.)
] <ex:mercer-exercise-brownian>

#exercise(title: [The classical proof of Mercer's theorem])[
  Prove @thm:mercer without using $H_K$, for a continuous Hermitian kernel $A$ with positive integral operator
  $T_A$, along the lines of @mercer1909:
  (a) $T_A$ maps $L^2(mu)$ into $C(X)$; hence the eigenfunctions have continuous representatives, unique by
  @prop:mercer-support.
  (b) The remainder $A_n (x, y) := A(x, y) - sum_(j <= n) lambda_j e_j (x) overline(e_j (y))$ is continuous and
  Hermitian, and $T_(A_n) = sum_(j > n) lambda_j ip(dot, e_j) e_j$ is positive. By @lem:mercer-positivity,
  $A_n$ is positive definite; conclude $sum_j lambda_j abs(e_j (x))^2 <= A(x, x)$.
  (c) For fixed $x$, the series $B(x, y) := sum_j lambda_j e_j (x) overline(e_j (y))$ converges uniformly in $y$,
  and $integral (B(x, y) - A(x, y)) g(y) dif mu(y) = 0$ for all $g in L^2(mu)$; deduce $B = A$.
  (d) Conclude with Dini's theorem and the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality as in the proof of @thm:mercer.
] <ex:mercer-exercise-classical>

#exercise(title: [The Poisson kernel])[
  On $TT$, let $w_k = rho^(abs(k))$ with $0 < rho < 1$. Show that
  $K(x, y) = (1 - rho^2) \/ (1 - 2 rho cos(x - y) + rho^2)$, and that every $f in H_K$ is the restriction to the
  unit circle of a function holomorphic in the annulus $rho^(1\/2) < abs(z) < rho^(-1\/2)$.
  _Hint:_ $abs(hat(f)(k)) <= norm(f)_(H_K) rho^(abs(k)\/2)$; consider $sum_k hat(f)(k) z^k$.
] <ex:mercer-exercise-poisson>

#exercise(title: [Sobolev weights])[
  Show that $sum_(k in ZZ^d) (1 + abs(k)^2)^(-s) < oo$ if and only if $s > d\/2$, by comparing the sum with
  $integral_(RR^d) (1 + abs(x)^2)^(-s) dif x$. Deduce the Sobolev embedding
  $norm(f)_oo <= C_(d, s) norm(f)_(H^s (TT^d))$ for $s > d\/2$ from @ex:mercer-torus.
] <ex:mercer-exercise-sobolev>

#exercise(title: [Measures without full support])[
  Let $(X, d)$ be compact metric, $mu$ a finite Borel measure, and $K$ continuous and positive definite, but
  do not assume $supp mu = X$. Let $X_0 := supp mu$.
  (a) Show that $X_0$ is compact and $mu(X without X_0) = 0$. _Hint:_ $X without X_0$ is a union of open null
  sets, and $X$ is second countable.
  (b) Show that the eigenpairs of $T_K$ on $L^2(X, mu)$ and of $T_(K_0)$ on $L^2(X_0, mu)$, with
  $K_0 = K|_(X_0 times X_0)$, correspond to each other, and deduce that @eq:mercer-expansion holds uniformly on
  $X_0 times X_0$ (use @prop:rkhs-sum-restriction for the RKHS of $K_0$).
  (c) Give an example where it fails at a point of $(X without X_0) times (X without X_0)$.
] <ex:mercer-exercise-support>

#exercise(title: [An indefinite kernel])[
  On $TT$ with $mu = dif x \/ (2 pi)$, let $A(x, y) = cos(x - y) - cos(2(x - y))$. Compute the eigenvalues of $T_A$
  and show that $T_A$ is not positive. Find two points $x_1, x_2$ for which the matrix
  $(A(x_i, x_l))_(i, l = 1)^2$ is not positive semidefinite, in accordance with @lem:mercer-positivity.
] <ex:mercer-exercise-indefinite>
